About This Campaign

This campaign focuses on raising awareness about security risks within the Web3 and cryptocurrency ecosystem.

The goal is to help beginners and everyday crypto users understand common threats and develop safer habits when interacting with blockchain applications.

Day 01 — Welcome to Web3 Security

I'm starting a 30-day journey to explore an important part of Web3: SECURITY.

From wallet protection to phishing and crypto scams, I'll be sharing simple lessons to help us become safer in the Web3 space.

Don't make it easy for them.

Learn • Verify • Protect 🛡️

#Web3 #Cybersecurity

Day 02 — Protect Your Seed Phrase

Your seed phrase is not just another password.

It can give someone control over your wallet and funds.

Never share it with anyone, regardless of who they claim to be.

Keep it private. Keep it secure.

#Web3Security #CryptoSecurity

Day 03 — Fake Airdrops

Not every airdrop is an opportunity.

Some fake airdrops are designed to steal wallet information or trick users into approving malicious transactions.

Before connecting your wallet, verify the project through its official channels.

Free tokens shouldn't cost you your wallet.

#Web3 #CryptoSecurity

Day 04 — Phishing Attacks

A single click can expose your wallet to danger.

Scammers create fake websites and messages that imitate trusted Web3 platforms.

Always verify links before connecting your wallet or entering information.

Pause. Verify. Protect.

#Phishing #Web3Security

Day 05 — Wallet Security

Your wallet is your gateway to Web3.

Protect it with:

- A secure device.
- A strong password.
- Safe seed phrase storage.
- Careful transaction approvals.

Your wallet deserves more attention than just checking your balance.

#WalletSecurity #Web3

Day 06 — Malicious Signatures

Not every signature request is harmless.

Some malicious websites trick users into signing messages that could authorize dangerous actions.

Never sign something you don't understand.

If you don't understand it, don't sign it.

#Web3Security #Crypto

Day 07 — Fake Support Accounts

Someone sends you a DM claiming to be customer support?

Be careful.

Scammers often impersonate project administrators and support teams to steal seed phrases or private keys.

Legitimate support should never need your seed phrase.

Verify before you trust.

#CryptoSecurity #Web3

Day 08 — Check URLs Before Connecting

A fake website can look almost identical to the real one.

Before connecting your wallet:

- Check the domain.
- Verify official links.
- Avoid suspicious redirects.

One wrong URL can become an expensive mistake.

#Web3Security #OnlineSafety

Day 09 — Two-Factor Authentication

Protecting your exchange account with a password alone may not be enough.

Enable two-factor authentication (2FA) wherever available.

It adds another layer of protection during login.

Security is better when you don't rely on just one layer.

#CryptoSecurity #2FA

Day 10 — Public Wi-Fi and Crypto

Using public Wi-Fi while accessing financial accounts requires caution.

Avoid sensitive transactions on networks you don't trust.

Use secure connections and keep your devices updated.

Convenience should never replace security.

#Web3Security #Cybersecurity

Day 11 — Urgency Is a Warning Sign

"Act now or lose your opportunity!"

Scammers often create urgency to stop people from thinking carefully.

Whether it's an investment, airdrop, or wallet notification, take time to verify.

Never let pressure make your security decisions.

#CryptoScams #Web3Security

Day 12 — Fake Giveaways

"Send 0.1 ETH and receive 1 ETH back."

Sounds attractive, right?

But fake crypto giveaways use promises of free money to steal funds.

Remember: sending cryptocurrency to an unknown address is generally irreversible.

Think before you send.

#CryptoSecurity #ScamAwareness

Day 13 — Email Security

Your email account can be an important gateway to your crypto accounts.

Protect it with:

- A unique password.
- Two-factor authentication.
- Careful attachment handling.
- Suspicious-link awareness.

Secure your email. Protect your digital identity.

#Cybersecurity #Web3Security

Day 14 — Don't Trust a Blue Tick Alone

A verification badge doesn't automatically make an account trustworthy.

Scammers can impersonate popular projects, founders, and influencers.

Check account history, official links, and announcements before taking action.

Verify the person, not just the badge.

#Web3 #CryptoSecurity

Day 15 — Smart Contract Awareness

Smart contracts make many Web3 applications possible.

But not every smart contract is safe.

Vulnerabilities, poor implementation, and malicious contract code can expose users to serious risks.

Understand what you're interacting with before committing funds.

#SmartContracts #Web3Security

Day 16 — Token Approvals

Connecting your wallet and approving token spending are not always the same thing.

Some applications request permission to spend tokens.

Review approval requests carefully and avoid granting unnecessary permissions.

Understand what you're approving.

#WalletSecurity #DeFi

Day 17 — Separate Your Wallets

Consider using different wallets for different activities.

For example:

- A wallet for long-term holdings.
- A wallet for everyday interactions.
- A separate wallet for testing unfamiliar applications.

This can help limit exposure if one wallet is compromised.

#Web3Security #WalletSecurity

Day 18 — Hardware Wallets

A hardware wallet can provide an additional layer of protection by keeping private keys isolated from internet-connected devices.

However, it doesn't eliminate every risk.

Always protect your recovery phrase and verify transactions before approving them.

#CryptoSecurity #HardwareWallet


Day 19 — Fake Crypto Applications

Not every application claiming to be a crypto wallet or exchange is legitimate.

Fake apps may imitate trusted platforms to steal credentials or funds.

Download applications from trusted sources and verify the developer.

Be careful what you install.

#Web3Security #CryptoSafety
Day 20 — Private Keys

Your private key gives control over your crypto assets.

Anyone who obtains it may be able to access your funds.

Never share it through messages, emails, websites, or forms.

Your private key belongs to you alone.

#PrivateKey #CryptoSecurity

Day 21 — QR Code Scams

QR codes are convenient, but they can also direct users to malicious websites or incorrect payment addresses.

Before scanning or approving a crypto transaction, verify where the QR code leads.

Convenience should not remove caution.

#Web3Security #CryptoSafety

Day 22 — Investment Scams

Guaranteed profits. No risk. Double your money.

These are common warning signs of investment scams.

Research projects carefully and be skeptical of unrealistic promises.

Never invest simply because someone online tells you to.

#CryptoScams #DYOR

Day 23 — Social Engineering in Web3

Not every attack requires technical skills.

Sometimes attackers manipulate people into giving away access or approving dangerous transactions.

They may pretend to be friends, project teams, or trusted community members.

Be alert. Verify identities. Never share sensitive information.

#SocialEngineering #Web3Security

Day 24 — Suspicious Files and Downloads

Be careful with files shared through Telegram, Discord, emails, and unfamiliar websites.

Some downloads may contain malicious software designed to compromise your device.

Don't install files simply because someone in a crypto community recommends them.

#Cybersecurity #Web3Security

Day 25 — Backups and Recovery

What happens if you lose access to your wallet or device?

Understanding wallet recovery is important.

Keep recovery information secure and maintain appropriate backups.

Never store your seed phrase carelessly or share it with anyone.

#WalletSecurity #CryptoSecurity

Day 26 — DYOR: Do Your Own Research

Before interacting with a Web3 project, take time to research it.

Look into:

- Official website and social accounts.
- Project documentation.
- Team information.
- Community discussions.
- Security audits, where available.

An audit is not a guarantee of safety, but research helps you make more informed decisions.

#DYOR #Web3

Day 27 — AI-Powered Crypto Scams

Artificial intelligence is creating new opportunities, but scammers can also misuse it.

AI-generated voices, images, and messages can make impersonation more convincing.

Don't trust something simply because it looks or sounds real.

Verify through independent, official channels.

#AI #Cybersecurity #Web3Security

Day 28 — What If Your Wallet Is Compromised?

If you suspect your wallet has been compromised, act quickly.

- Stop interacting with suspicious applications.
- Use a clean, trusted device.
- Consider moving remaining assets to a new secure wallet if safe to do so.
- Review and revoke risky token approvals where applicable.
- Report the incident through appropriate channels.

Never share your recovery phrase while seeking help.

#IncidentResponse #CryptoSecurity

Day 29 — Build Better Security Habits

Web3 security isn't something you practise only when something goes wrong.

Make security part of your daily routine.

Verify links. Protect your seed phrase. Review approvals. Research projects. Be careful with signatures.

Small habits can help prevent costly mistakes.

#Web3Security #CyberAwareness

Day 30 — Campaign Reflection

30 days of Web3 security awareness. 

We've explored wallet security, seed phrases, phishing, fake airdrops, malicious signatures, smart contracts, scams, and much more.

One lesson stands out:

Being part of Web3 means taking responsibility for your own security.

Don't rush. Don't trust blindly. Always verify.

Learn • Verify • Protect.

Thank you for following this journey.

